// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row boxdyn_double_deref_wrong_error_code (tier 4, `diag`).
// The VERDICT is right and the SENTENCE names the wrong rule: `use_ref(&**b)` with
// `b: Box<dyn Sp>` is refused, but with this round's unsize diagnostic
//   error [fn main]: the trait bound `Box<dyn Sp>: Sp` is not satisfied — required for
//   the cast from `&Box<dyn Sp>` to `&dyn Sp`; … borrow the pointee instead: `&*<expr>` (E0277)
// where rustc 1.98.1 refuses it as a DEREF error, not an unsize one:
//   error[E0614]: type `dyn Sp` cannot be dereferenced
// (twin: src/compiler/probes/2026-09-15h-boxdynref/rust/w1_double_deref_arg.rs).
// The advice is also wrong for this program: the cure is to DROP a `*`, not to add one.
//
// ROOT, read 2026-09-15h: Logos represents `&dyn Tr` and `dyn Tr` as the SAME
// Kind::TraitObject/Borrow, so `*b`'s result is type-indistinguishable from a `&dyn Tr`
// binding, and `&**b` is type-indistinguishable from the LEGAL reborrow `&*r` where
// `r: &dyn Tr`. Emitting E0614 therefore needs a representation that separates the
// unsized place `dyn Tr` from the reference `&dyn Tr` — not a new check at this site.
// On base binary d8d756468efca2d6 43 this program died as `mlir_gen: internal: no vtable`;
// it is a refusal either way, so this row records a SENTENCE defect, never an admission.
package boxdyn_double_deref_wrong_error_code;
use logos.mem.boxed;

trait Sp { fn v(&self) -> i64; }
struct A { x: i64 }
impl Sp for A { fn v(&self) -> i64 { return self.x; } }

fn use_ref(r: &dyn Sp) -> i64 { return r.v(); }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let b: Box<dyn Sp> = box_new(A { x: 42i64 });
    if use_ref(&**b) != 42i64 { return 3i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
